//! The page's prose, for a reader with new eyes: one who knows neither
//! geometry nor machine learning nor X_eTaL. `[shown|id]` marks a word
//! that opens its plain explanation (docs/glossary.md, the pages'
//! glossary); `**words**` is bold. Kept apart from the page so a test
//! can check every mark names an idea the glossary has and no sentence
//! passes a verdict. No result lives here: the numbers are the run's.

pub const LEDE: &str = "One bowl-shaped loss that does not change, and three rulers for measuring a step down it. The ruler changes the direction called downhill, the path taken and the number of steps to the floor, while the bowl stays the bowl. Then the same question on a curved space: a step that stays on the sphere beside a step that leaves it and is pushed back.";

pub const HINT: &str = "New to this? A word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation when you click it (or press Enter on it); click again to close it. The buttons pick a, the dial of the third ruler diag(a, 1), which the page calls the control; picking runs nothing, every number was computed by the program at the bottom when the page opened. The picture of the sphere turns when you drag it, press the arrow keys once it has the focus, or swipe; turning changes no number.";

pub const QUESTION: [&str; 3] = [
    "Is downhill one direction? When training lowers a loss by stepping against its gradient, is the direction of the step a fact about the loss, or a choice?",
    "It is a choice. The gradient points the way the loss rises fastest per unit of distance, and \"distance\" is a [metric|metric], a ruler laid on the space of the model's numbers. Change the ruler and the steepest direction changes, though the loss and its contours do not move. An optimizer that scales each weight's step on its own (Adam, a widely used one, does, with running averages, so its ruler changes as it goes), a [natural-gradient|natural-gradient] method, a [preconditioner|preconditioner]: each is a change of ruler. This page keeps its rulers constant.",
    "This page shows it on a loss you can see whole, a bowl in two numbers x and y: the same start, the same way of choosing how far to go, three rulers, three paths, three step counts. One direction is worked in full under two rulers. And since the research this repository follows warns that the formula for a step under a metric is only the step in coordinates, and that a curved space needs a way back onto it, the last part takes the question onto the sphere.",
];

pub const BACKGROUND: [&str; 5] = [
    "A **loss** is a number that says how badly a model is doing; training lowers it. Here the model has two numbers, x and y, and the loss is L(x, y) = (x^2 + 3 y^2) / 2: a bowl whose floor is at x = y = 0, three times as steep along y as along x. The space of (x, y) is the model's [parameter space|parameter-space]; the page draws it as a plane.",
    "A [contour|contour] is a curve along which the loss has one value, like a height line on a map; this bowl's contours are ellipses, squeezed along y. The **gradient** at a point is the pair of slopes (how fast L rises along x, along y), here (x, 3 y); drawn at every point it is a [gradient field|gradient-field], a field of arrows that all come from the one function L. Following the arrows backward is **gradient descent**.",
    "A [metric|metric] is a rule for the length of a small step v = (vx, vy): the plain ruler says sqrt(vx^2 + vy^2); a ruler written as a matrix G says sqrt(v' G v) (v' is v written as a row, so v' G v is one number; diag(a, 1) is the 2 by 2 matrix with a and 1 on its diagonal and zeros elsewhere), which for G = diag(a, 1) is sqrt(a vx^2 + vy^2), a ruler that counts x-steps a times as heavily. The set of steps of length 1 under a ruler is its **unit ball**: a circle for the plain ruler, an ellipse for the others.",
    "A step under a metric is p <- p - eta P g, with g the gradient, P the inverse of G (the [preconditioner|preconditioner]) and eta the step size. With G the identity it is the plain step. The step goes against P g: -P g is the direction, within G's unit ball, along which L falls fastest, so steepest descent is relative to the ruler.",
    "The **step size policy** is how far to go along the chosen direction. Here it is an exact [line search|line-search]: the one number eta that makes the loss least along the line, which this bowl gives in closed form. So the ruler chooses the direction and nothing else, and a ruler and twice that ruler give the same path (the control's a = 1/3, which is H / 3, takes the same one step as H). (With a fixed eta instead, doubling G would halve every step: the ruler's size and the step size are one choice, which the next part of this explorer, training a real model, meets.)",
];

pub const RATIONALE: [&str; 2] = [
    "Keep the loss fixed, change only the ruler, and measure what changes: the direction of the first step, the shape of the path, the steps to reach a target loss. If the paths differ while the contours stay put, downhill is not one direction, and a method's choice of ruler is a choice of geometry that costs something (the numbers in G, the multiplies in P g) and may buy something (fewer steps).",
    "The sharpest ruler to try is the bowl's own curvature, G = H = diag(1, 3): under it the bowl is round, every direction is equally steep, and the floor is one step away (Newton's direction). The ruler diag(a, 1) with a dialed from small to large passes through that choice at a = 1/3 and shows what each side of it does.",
];

pub const PURPOSE: [&str; 2] = [
    "This page is an experiment on a toy loss, not on a model: the loss is a formula, so every path, every count and the worked direction can be checked by hand, and the gradient is checked against central differences on the page. Every number, point and curve comes from the X_eTaL program shown at the bottom, run in your browser when the page opened; the buttons choose which of the program's numbers you read and run nothing.",
    "It compares three rulers on one loss, with the same start and the same step size policy; and on the sphere, two ways of taking a step under a constraint. The figures are marked [measured or analytical|measured-analytical]: the paths, counts, directions and the eigenvector are measured; the contours, the unit balls and the sphere's wire lines are analytical, shapes drawn from their formulas (every point of them still computed by the program).",
];

pub const INTUITION: [&str; 3] = [
    "A long narrow valley, its floor sloping gently to a lake. (On the bowl below the valley runs along x, the gentle direction, and is narrow along y, the steep one.) Standing on the valley's side, the steepest way down is straight toward the floor, across the valley, not along it toward the lake. A walker who takes the steepest step each time crosses the floor, climbs a little up the far side, turns, crosses back: a zigzag that reaches the lake slowly. The zigzag is not a mistake of the walker; it is what \"steepest\" means with an ordinary ruler.",
    "Now give the walker a ruler that counts sideways steps as long and lengthwise steps as short. Under that ruler the valley is not narrow, and the steepest direction, measured by it, points down the valley toward the lake. Same valley, same walker, same rule (go the steepest way), a different path. That ruler is the metric; choosing it is choosing the geometry the walker measures by.",
    "On the sphere there is a further catch: a step along the gradient's arrow leaves the surface, since the arrow is straight and the surface is not. Either you step along the surface (the [exponential map|exponential-map]: an arc), or you step straight and are pushed back onto the surface afterward (a retraction). The page measures how far the straight step leaves the sphere before it is pushed back, and whether the two ways arrive at the same place.",
];

pub const MATHEMATICS: [&str; 6] = [
    "The loss L(x, y) = (x^2 + 3 y^2) / 2. Its gradient is g = (x, 3 y): the slope along x is x, the slope along y is 3 y. Its curvature is the constant matrix H = diag(1, 3) (the Hessian: the slopes' own slopes), which is what makes the contours ellipses with the y axis the short one. The program checks g against central differences (the slope estimated from the loss a little to each side), (L(p + h e) - L(p - h e)) / 2 h with h = 1e-5 and e a unit step along x or along y, at three points.",
    "A metric G (a symmetric matrix with positive eigenvalues) gives a step v the length sqrt(v' G v). The direction of steepest descent under G at a point with gradient g is -P g, with P the inverse of G; scaled to length 1 under G it is v = -P g / sqrt(g' P g) (g . v is the dot product gx vx + gy vy; |g| is g's length, sqrt(g . g)). Why: among all v with v' G v = 1, the one with the smallest g . v is that one (a multiplier argument, the standard way to find the extreme point under a constraint; the page checks it by trying 360 directions). The rate of fall along it, g . v, is a slope per unit of G's length. A length-1 step under H is a different step from a length-1 step under I (the two unit balls in the figure are different shapes), so a rate per plain unit and a rate per H unit are not one number smaller than the other, as miles per hour and miles per gallon are not; the page gives both and compares neither.",
    "The update p <- p - eta P g. The three rulers: G = I, the plain step, P g = g; G = diag(a, 1), P g = (gx / a, gy); G = H, P g = (x, y), the point itself, so one step of eta = 1 reaches the floor from anywhere: Newton's direction. The exact line search along d = P g on this bowl: eta = (g . d) / (d' H d), the minimum of the quadratic L(p - eta d), in closed form (one formula, no searching). When d is zero (the floor) the step is zero.",
    "Under the plain ruler with an exact line search each step is at right angles to the one before (the search stops where the new gradient has no part along the old direction), which is the zigzag; the loss falls by at least a fixed factor per step that depends on the bowl's [condition number|condition-number], the ratio of its steepest to its gentlest curvature, 3 here. Under diag(a, 1) the bowl's condition number, measured by that ruler, becomes the larger of 3 a and 1 / (3 a) (the page says the ruler \"leaves\" the bowl with it): 1 at a = 1/3, where diag(a, 1) is H / 3, the same direction as Newton's.",
    "On the sphere: the quotient f(p) = p' A p for a symmetric 3 by 3 matrix A (written below by rows, (4 1 0 / 1 3 1 / 0 1 2)), among unit vectors p, is largest at A's leading [eigenvector|eigenvector], and its value there is the largest eigenvalue (Rayleigh's quotient, after the physicist who used it). The gradient of f in the room is 2 A p; the [Riemannian gradient|riemannian-gradient] on the unit sphere is that arrow's part along the sphere, 2 A p - 2 (p' A p) p (the [tangent vector|tangent-vector] at p). A step that stays on the sphere goes along it by the exponential map, cos t p + sin t v / t with t the arrow's length; the flat step p + 2 eta A p leaves the sphere by about the arrow's length and is scaled back to length 1.",
    "The program's words for these: u:l_oss and u:g_rad (the bowl), u:e_ta (the line search), u:s_tep (the update under P; a step under a metric is a function asked of the sibling library X_eTaL-ML, ask GM4, and written out here meanwhile), s_phereDescend and s_phereRetract (Manifold's, this repository's), e_igenvectors (Matrix's, X_eTaL-libraries', found by cyclic Jacobi, a standard method, for the check). The contours are the ellipses' own formula, since a general contour finder is asked of X_eTaL-libraries (ask GL6).",
];

pub const EXPERIMENT: [&str; 3] = [
    "What is measured. On the bowl: the path under each ruler from the same start (the preset's), as points, for a fixed number of steps; the loss at every step; the steps to a loss under the preset's target; the first step worked (its direction, its eta, the point reached). The control: the ruler diag(a, 1) at eight values of a, 1/9, 1/6, 1/3, 1/2, 1, 2, 3 and 9 (four below Newton's 1/3 and four above, chosen so that the pairs a and 1 / (9 a) leave the same condition number), each a full descent, with the condition number each leaves, the steps to the target, the loss after five steps and at the end. The steepest direction at the start under the plain ruler and under H, with each ruler's unit ball and the rate of fall along each. On the sphere: the three paths (the exact step, the retraction, the flat step repaired) from the same start at the same rate; per step the quotient, the distance along the sphere to the eigenvector, and how far off the sphere the step went before any repair; the steps to a distance under the tolerance; the gradient of the quotient against central differences; the eigenvector and eigenvalues from Matrix for the check.",
    "Held equal: the loss, the start, the step count, the step size policy (the exact line search) on the bowl; the matrix A, the start, the rate and the step count on the sphere. What differs: the metric; and on the sphere, how the step is kept on it.",
    "Written before the run: what would count against \"downhill depends on the ruler\" is the three paths coinciding, or the step counts agreeing within one. What would count for Newton's direction is reaching the target in one step where the plain step takes several; what would count against the step that stays on the sphere is the flat step arriving at the same place in the same number of steps, since then the repair costs nothing visible on this problem; and likewise the retraction against the exact step: if the cheap step arrives in the same count, the exact step's sine and cosine bought nothing here (either would be a result, and the page would say so).",
];

pub const RUN: [&str; 1] = [
    "No model is trained: the run is gradient descent on a formula, which is the training step with the model removed. Each descent is a fixed number of steps of p <- p - eta P g with eta from the line search; each climb on the sphere a fixed number of steps at a fixed rate. The page reads the paths and the logs back; the buttons choose which value of a to show.",
];

pub const ANALYSIS: [&str; 3] = [
    "The comparison on the bowl: for each ruler, the steps to the target loss, the loss after one step and after five, the loss at the end, the numbers the ruler is made of and the multiplies a step spends on P g. The control's table gives the same for diag(a, 1) at every a, with the condition number each leaves the bowl with. For the sphere: the quotient and the distance to the eigenvector at the end, the steps to the tolerance, and the farthest and the mean distance off the sphere a step went before its repair.",
    "How to read the step counts: the plain ruler zigzags and takes its steps; Newton's takes one; the control falls toward a = 1/3 from both sides and rises away from it. Two values of a that leave the same condition number (a and 1 / (9 a)) take about as many steps, not the same: the condition number bounds the rate, the start sets where within the bound. A smaller condition number is not free: diag(a, 1) costs one number and two multiplies a step; H is costed as a general symmetric matrix, three numbers and four multiplies, since on a model the curvature is not diagonal (on this bowl it happens to be, and would cost two and two); and on a model with many weights a full H is out of reach, which is why the next part of this explorer tries a diagonal and an approximate natural gradient on a real model and counts what each costs.",
    "On the sphere, the three ways arrive at the same eigenvector (the distance to Matrix's is under the tolerance for all three); what differs is the steps they take and how far from the sphere they pass on the way. The rate is the same number for all three, not the same movement along the sphere: the flat step spends part of its arrow off the surface (the table's third number), which the repair throws away. The flat step is, in fact, the power iteration of linear algebra (p scaled by I + 2 eta A each step), which near the eigenvector shrinks the distance by the ratio of that matrix's top two eigenvalues each step; the step along the sphere shrinks it by 1 - 2 eta (lambda1 - lambda2). Both factors are analytical, from the eigenvalues, and the page gives them with the counts they predict for this rate; the page measures one rate only. Read against what was written before the run: the retraction arrived in the same count as the exact step and at the same end distance, so on this problem the exact step's sine and cosine bought nothing over the cheap step; that is a result, and the table's distances at step 1 show where the two differ (in the third decimal) and where they agree (at the end).",
];

pub const EXTENSION: [&str; 3] = [
    "What this rests on (docs/papers.md): the research's own picture of a change of metric (same loss, metric diag(a, 1), the same start), which this page reproduces with the control; its caution that p - eta inverse(G) g is the step in coordinates and a curved space also needs a retraction or an exponential map, which the sphere part shows; Absil, Mahony and Sepulchre's chapter 4 for the retraction and the step on the sphere; Amari for the natural gradient, which the next part tries. The exact line search on a quadratic and the right-angle zigzag are textbook (any numerical optimization text's steepest-descent chapter).",
    "Open, for this repository: whether a metric pays for itself on a model, where the loss is not a bowl and its curvature changes from point to point and is too large to hold (metric-training, the next step: plain, a diagonal preconditioner, a natural-gradient approximation on the same data and seeds, steps to a target and the cost of each step counted). And H3: whether a metric learned with the model can beat one chosen for it, and whether it can deceive.",
    "What this page does not show: a loss with more than one floor (the line search would still find a floor, maybe not the lowest), a step size that is fixed rather than searched (then the ruler's size matters, and a ruler too sharp for the step size diverges), or a ruler that changes from point to point (the Fisher metric does; here every ruler is constant).",
];

pub const SUMMARY: [&str; 3] = [
    "Downhill is not one direction. On one bowl that did not move, three rulers gave three first steps, three paths and three step counts; the ruler of the bowl's own curvature made the floor one step away, and the dial a of diag(a, 1) showed the steps falling toward that ruler from both sides and rising away from it. The steepest direction under a ruler is the point of that ruler's unit ball where the loss falls fastest, and the unit balls differ.",
    "On the sphere, a step along the surface and a straight step pushed back both reached the leading eigenvector; the straight step left the sphere by a measured amount before each repair and took more steps, and the retraction matched the exact step in count and in end distance, which by the rule written before the run says the exact step bought nothing over the cheap one here.",
    "For machine learning: an optimizer's preconditioner is a ruler, a choice of geometry on the parameter space; it changes the path and the count, not the loss, and it costs numbers and multiplies per step. Whether it pays on a real model is the next part's question, not this one's.",
];

